{"answer":"The critic's case conflates two distinct computations and overstates what is established:\n\n1. **'Inherently dependent on the entry point'** — Conceded only partially: the traversal *starts* from the target, but a fixed traversal order (deterministic priority over attack/defense edges, tie-broken by node identifier) makes the order reproducible from any entry point that reaches the same subgraph. The critic asserts dependence on 'previously memoized node states' but does not establish that memoization introduces *nondeterminism*; a memo table keyed by node identity returns the same value regardless of insertion timing. What the critic actually shows is that the traversal order may *vary* with entry point, not that it is *unfixed*.\n\n2. **'Recursive dependence on the target's state causes cycles'** — True for cyclic graphs in general, but the target specifies expansion 'up to the first fixpoint hit' and memoization of visited labels. Cycle handling is a standard local fixpoint within the reachable subgraph; it does not require resolving the *global* fixed point unless the reachable subgraph happens to be the full graph. The critic has not established that cycle resolution on the subgraph is equivalent to the global fixed-point in all cases—only that it can coincide when the subgraph is large.\n\n3. **'Contradicting the goal of semantically novel lazy evaluation'** — Not established. Semantic novelty does not require a different *result* on every graph; it requires a different *evaluation regime*. Computing labels on a reachable subgraph with memoization and certifying by polynomial path-check is a different regime from batch full-graph iteration, even if results coincide. The critic has not shown the results *must* always coincide; the certification check verifies omitted nodes cannot affect the target, which is a strictly weaker condition than full extension computation when the reachable subgraph is proper.\n\n4. **'The certification check is a fixed-point computation in disguise'** — The check is a polynomial reachability test over undefeated attacker paths to the target, which is a graph search (path existence under a label condition), not a label-fixpoint iteration. It does not recompute node labels; it verifies a separation property. Calling it 'fixed-point in disguise' is unsupported unless the critic shows the reachability test necessarily computes all labels, which it has not.\n\n5. **'May merely re-derive the full extension in the worst case'** — Conceded as a worst-case possibility (the target itself acknowledges worst-case polynomial), but 'may' is not 'must.' The critic has not established that the worst case is typical or that the bookkeeping is pure overhead in sub-linear-practice cases. The cost-profile change claim is about practice, not asymptotic worst case, and remains unrefuted.\n\n**Summary:** The critic establishes that (a) traversal order can depend on entry point in principle and (b) worst-case full-subgraph expansion is possible. The critic does *not* establish that the fixed traversal order is nondeterministic, that cycle handling requires global fixed-point equivalence, that the certification check is a disguised full fixed-point, or that the lazy evaluation adds no semantic or practical value. The target's claim survives with the acknowledgment that worst-case behavior can match full computation—a point the target already concedes."}